


----

Scheduled — you will see two types of schedule type Simple and Advanced

Simple: This provides options for scheduling periodic job runs on a daily, hourly, or weekly basis.
Advanced: This option allows you to schedule jobs using CRON syntax for precise timing.
Table Update - A trigger can be set up in platforms like Azure Databricks to run automatically whenever one or more specified tables are updated.

Continuous — runs repeatedly with a short interval between runs.

File arrival — monitors an external location or volume for new files. 
Note the Advanced settings, where you can adjust the time between checks and the delay after a new file arrives before starting a run.


----


As a part of ingestion, you can perform inserts, updates and deletes using data from a source table, view, or DataFrame into a target Delta table using the MERGE SQL operation.
Delta Lake supports inserts, updates and deletes in MERGE, and supports extended syntax beyond the SQL standards to facilitate advanced use cases.

Syntax:
MERGE INTO target t
USING source s
ON {merge_condition}
WHEN MATCHED THEN {matched_action}
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN {not_matched_action}

if any chnage in schema or to adding new column then when unsing merge use as
MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO main_users_target target .....  -- Use the MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO statement
and add the new column in last insert query.


DESCRIBE HISTORY main_users_target; -> to get the histroy on tables

You can use VERSION AS OF to query a specific version of the table. Query version 1 of the table to view the original data.

eg:
MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO main_users_target target  -- Use the MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO statement
USING new_users_source source
ON target.id = source.id
WHEN MATCHED AND source.status = 'update' THEN
  UPDATE SET 
    target.email = source.email,
    target.status = source.status
WHEN MATCHED AND source.status = 'delete' THEN
  DELETE
WHEN NOT MATCHED AND source.status = 'new' THEN
  INSERT (id, first_name, email, sign_up_date, status, country)
  VALUES (source.id, source.first_name, source.email, source.sign_up_date, source.status, source.country);

----

Schema Enforcement – STRUCT columns define and enforce a schema, helping maintain data integrity.
Improved Performance – STRUCTs are generally more efficient for querying and processing than plain strings.

Schema_of_json()-> https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/schema_of_json
eg:
SELECT schema_of_json('{"device":"Linux","ecommerce":{"purchase_revenue_in_usd":1075.5,"total_item_quantity":1,"unique_items":1} as schema

from_json() - >This function parses a column containing a JSON-formatted string into a STRUCT type using the specified schema for a new table.

We can query the STRUCT column using value.device or value.ecommerce in our SELECT statement.



---
Base64 encoding is commonly used when ingesting data from sources like message queues or streaming platforms,
where preserving formatting and avoiding data corruption is important.
(Values will be in encrypt format when ingest)


%sql
SELECT
  key AS encoded_key,
  unbase64(key) AS decoded_key,
  value AS encoded_value,
  unbase64(value) AS decoded_value
FROM kafka_events_bronze_raw
LIMIT 5;

-- String to binary type when encoded with unbase64()
--Run the cell and view the results. Notice that the decoded_key and decoded_value columns are now BINARY



----
Rescued Data Column
The rescued data column ensures that rows that don’t match with the schema are rescued instead of being dropped. 
The rescued data column contains any data that isn’t parsed for the following reasons:

The column is missing from the schema.
Type mismatches
Case mismatches

what is other cases like

you use spark.read.json, spark.read.csv, or LOAD DATA then
If the schema doesn’t match the data (e.g., wrong types), Spark may throw errors or set values to NULL.


SELECT *
FROM read_files(
        DA.paths_working_dir || '/csv_demo_files/malformed_example_1_data.csv',
        format => "csv",
        sep => "|",
        header => true,
        schema => '''
            order_id INT, 
            email STRING, 
            transactions_timestamp BIGINT''', 
        rescueddatacolumn => '_rescued_data'    -- Create the _rescued_data column
      );

--
TO ingest the rescuded data in the column for the table
SELECT
  cast(_rescued_data:_c0 AS BIGINT) AS order_id from table_name;

-----

eg:

select
CAST(
  COALESCE( 
REPLACE(get_json_object(_rescued_data,'$.price'),"$",''), 
'0'
) as DOUBLE
 )as all_price,
  * from 05_lab_bronze;

--
get_json_object() is the most common way to pull values out of a JSON string

With read_files() → extra fields are preserved in _rescued_data.

Without read_files() → extra fields are dropped, unless you explicitly parse the raw JSON yourself.


---
Metadata - https://docs.databricks.com/en/ingestion/file-metadata-column.html
To access metadata:
the DESCRIBE DETAIL command is specifically used to return metadata about a Delta table. 
Unlike DESCRIBE HISTORY (which shows the transaction log of operations like MERGE, UPDATE, OPTIMIZE, etc.), 
DESCRIBE DETAIL gives you the current state and properties of the table.
eg:
DESCRIBE DETAIL 'location_of_the_file'
some details in metadata can be take like _metadata.file_modification_time, _metadata.file_name ...


